                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "It's definitely for more experienced and/or motivated puzzlers, It is a masterpiece though.\r\n\r\nA few comments. There are some puzzles early in the game in Larva and/or Whale only stages that are very challenging and have exceptional craftsmanship and creativity. But then in the late game the difficult generally drop to what should have been the early to mid game difficulties. So it is kind of odd to go through a big struggle with the first handful of stages only to find the difficult dropping and rarely climbing back to anything close to those first stages. I feel like that's partly why this game gets such an interesting reputation, for better or worse. Some of the levels in the first few stages are real humdingers and you basically just started playing and it makes quite an impression.\r\n\r\nAnd last couple of things is related to those same early Larva and/or whale stages. The gravity mechanic is a bit unclear/confusing and a better explanation would have went a long way imo. Especially because you can swing your square through a 270 degree arc before landing but push a larva or whale in the 90 to 180 portion of the swing and the gravity imbued is given by the orientation of the square immediately after the push at the 180 mark, and NOT the orientation when the swing move stops at the 270 mark!!\r\n\r\nThis next part is going to be a bit of a spoiler for people just starting the game so don't unspoiler if you don't want any hints yet.\r\n\r\n[spoiler] There are some specific puzzles where the main point is to find a neat trick in the puzzle mechanics, but I feel like the extended consequences of the base mechanics is somewhat arbitrary. Specifically, when pushing one object into another object, that second object inherits the same gravity as the first object even though your square does not touch it. That was the most unintuitive one for me. [/spoiler]\r\n\r\nBy the time I got to the Freezer stage and a clever trick is introduced I was fine with it because I had already figured out that you can't assume what order things will happen in during a swing of the square. And just in general I feel like I had gotten partway into the head of the developer and my intuition started to line up with the actual solutions more closely. But only after a dozen hours of intense brain exercise and a couple of brief existential crises.\r\n\r\nI'm not sure if the difficult to phase of the game relationship was skewed towards difficult first intentionally or not. Perhaps to showcase the finest puzzle designs relatively early and/or as a flex in terms of challenge. But in any case my mild complaints really only serve to enhance the mythology of this game.\r\n\r\nStephen Lavelle is a legend. Thanks for this one. I'm sad I'll never be able to play it for the first time again.",
